# Braess paradox network.
#
# Four nodes, two routes of equal cost, plus a tempting shortcut in the
# middle. With enough Agents the shortcut raises the average travel time,
# which is the classic counter-example of adding a road that makes everyone
# worse off. Watch the Traffic panel: the relative gap and the total travel
# time tell whether the assignment has settled, and the heatmap shows the
# shortcut filling first.

resources
	resource People
end

paths
	path Road color 0xAAAAAA
end

segments
	segment Fast color 0x55CC55 speed 80 capacity 8 beta 4
	segment Slow color 0xCC5555 speed 20 capacity 40 beta 4
	segment Shortcut color 0xCCCC55 speed 200 capacity 6 beta 4
end

agents
	agent Worker color 0xFFFFFF speed 40
end

rules
	unitRule Send
		rate 4
		local People remove 1
		agent Worker to End add [ People 1 ]
	end

	unitRule Absorb
		rate 1
		local People greater 0
		local People remove 1
	end
end

units
	unit Start color 0x00FF00 mapRadius 1 rules [ Send ] targets [ Start ] caps [ People 200 ] resources [ People 80 ]
	unit End color 0xFF0000 mapRadius 1 rules [ Absorb ] targets [ End ] caps [ People 200 ] resources [ ]
end

maps
	map People color 0xFFFF00 capacity 10 rules [ ]
end
